  "curriculum": "The 2026–2027 OER for Nederlandse Talen en Culturen identifies Frisian Language and Culture as the separate, target-named FTC track. Articles 4.1.1b and 5.1.1c close at 60 EC per year. The first year combines the shared literature and linguistics units with Frisian Studies, standard language and use, and a faculty choice. Year 2 combines Frisian and shared study. The autumn Year 3 minor is followed by a faculty choice, the thesis and an internship, worth 10 EC each. Do not stack predecessor cohorts, the Dutch track or the separate Frisian minor. The OER explicitly defers the new cohort’s Year 2 and Year 3 listings to their later catalogue years. Dutch or Frisian are the track languages, with English permissible for some courses.",
  "progression": "The first year combines foundations in sound systems, syntax, semantics, cognition and interpretation with entry into Frisian studies. Year 2 deepens language use, literature, regional study, language acquisition and research. The OER requires ateliers in both literature and linguistics. Article 7.1 requires at least 50 EC from year one, 100 EC in the BA and completion of Research Approaches before the thesis for this cohort. The full first two years and Research Approaches meet the requirement before the spring thesis. Article 3.2 calls for the language of the track for the thesis and permits a different language outside Dutch or Frisian only with board permission. The internship is a separate 10 EC component.",
